Definition:
    -The observer pattern defines one to many dependency between objects so that
     when one object changes state the dependent objects are notified and updated
     about the change
    -The object which is being observed by other objects is called as the subject or
     observable, the dependent or subscribed objects are called as observers




How to do it:
    -So we mainly have 2 interfaces, One interface is the Subject interface in which we have the 3 main methods
     that is the registerObservers(), removeObservers(), notifyObservers().
     these three are implemented by concrete classes as per the requirements.
    -The other interface is the Observer interface which has one method, update(), here i have implemented two methods
     pushUpdate and pullUpdate, pushUpdate is the method where the subject pushes the state changes, in pullUpdate
     the subject sends itself and then the observer pulls the data, that's the only main difference. But the
     main idea is that the subject calls the observers update method during the notify method.


Why observer pattern:
    -Well observer pattern helps in defining loose coupling architecture where if two objects need to know some info
     about one another then they need not be too interdependent on each other
    -mainly used in such scenarios where state change in one object needs to be known by other objects
    -used in UI buttons, used in Spring application context, used in messaging systems, notification systems



